# Access Routes at Scale — SCRAPE and VENDOR Sources

Companion to `JOB_BOARD_INVENTORY.md` and `JOB_SCRAPERS_PLAN.md`.

## The reframe

Your scale problem feels hard because the inventory implicitly treats "SCRAPE" as meaning "point a browser at it." For hostile sources, direct browser scraping is the **worst** route on every axis simultaneously: highest cost per posting, lowest reliability, weakest legal footing, and worst reproducibility — a corpus you cannot re-collect is a corpus a reviewer cannot verify.

There are four routes, and direct scraping is the last one. Work down this list per source and stop at the first that yields:

| # | Route | Scale ceiling | Cost | Load on target |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **A** | Archived crawls (Common Crawl / Web Data Commons) | Billions | ~Free | **Zero** |
| **B** | Published front door (sitemap + JSON-LD, RSS, backing JSON API) | Millions | Low | Low, and expected |
| **C** | Managed vendor collection | Millions | $$ | Vendor's problem |
| **D** | Direct scraping | Thousands–millions | High | High |

Routes A and B are not compromises. For most of your sources they return **more** data, **more** cheaply, in a **more** structured form than direct scraping, while touching the target's servers less or not at all.

---

## Route A — Archived crawls

### A.1 Web Data Commons JobPosting subset

This is the highest-leverage single resource in your entire project and it requires no scraping at all.

<cite index="127-1">The Web Data Commons project has extracted schema.org data from the Common Crawl every year since 2013 and offers it for public download as the schema.org data set series; the latest release consists of 106 billion RDF quads describing 3.1 billion entities from 12.8 million different websites.</cite> <cite index="128-1">Job postings are one of the popular annotated entity types, and class-specific subsets are created out of the merged Microdata and JSON-LD corpora for a selection of schema.org classes.</cite>

Concretely:
- **`JobPosting` is one of the published class-specific subsets.** <cite index="136-1">Class-specific subsets exist for 44 schema.org classes, with download formats in CSV and TSV alongside N-Quads, released against annual Common Crawl snapshots.</cite>
- <cite index="128-1">Data is in N-Quads, where the fourth element of each quad carries the URL of the page the data came from</cite> — so you get provenance per record for free, which is exactly what you need for country attribution and for the source-stratification your methodology depends on.
- Maintained by the Data and Web Science group at the University of Mannheim, citable, with a stable publication. A reviewer can reproduce your corpus.
- Annual releases going back to 2013 give you a **longitudinal** dimension you cannot buy at any price.

**What it gives you per posting:** `title`, `description` (the full text), `datePosted`, `validThrough`, `jobLocation`, `hiringOrganization`, and often `skills`, `qualifications`, `experienceRequirements`, `employmentType`, `baseSalary`.

**Do this first.** Download one release, filter the JobPosting subset to your target domains and countries, and measure what fraction of your desired corpus is already sitting there. My expectation is that it covers a large share of your US, European, Indian and South African needs outright.

**Limits, stated honestly:** Common Crawl is a sample, not a census — per-domain coverage is partial and varies. It respects robots.txt, so any board that blocks it is absent. Login-walled content is absent. Chinese domains are systematically under-crawled. And snapshots are annual, so you cannot study a specific month.

### A.2 Common Crawl directly, for gap-filling

When WDC's coverage of a specific domain is thin, go to Common Crawl itself rather than to the live site:

1. Query the **CC index API** (`index.commoncrawl.org`) by URL prefix — e.g. all archived URLs under `naukri.com/job-listings-*` for a given crawl.
2. The index returns WARC filename, offset and length per URL.
3. Fetch **only those byte ranges** from the CC S3 bucket via HTTP range requests.
4. You now have the original archived HTML of those pages, having sent zero requests to the target.

This works at genuine scale, costs almost nothing, and is the single most under-used technique in job-ad research. It also sidesteps the entire anti-bot question, because you are reading an archive, not visiting a site.

**Caveat:** you are bound by the crawl date. For a trend study this is a feature; for "what is being asked for right now" it is not.

---

## Route B — The published front door

Job boards *want* machine-readable access, because Google for Jobs demands it. They just do not advertise it as an API.

### B.1 Sitemap + JSON-LD

<cite index="122-1">Two things get listings onto Google for Jobs: JobPosting structured data (JSON-LD) on every job page, and XML sitemaps for discovery and updates.</cite> <cite index="126-1">Google ingests the entire sitemap and recrawls pages whose lastmod times are more recent than the last crawl.</cite> <cite index="123-1">Required properties are title, description, datePosted, validThrough, jobLocation with address, and hiringOrganization.</cite>

The `description` property carries the **complete job description as HTML**. That is precisely your deliverable, pre-extracted, with no DOM parsing and no risk of a CSS-selector change silently breaking your pipeline.

**Pipeline, per board:**

```
GET /robots.txt              → read Sitemap: directives, and the crawl rules
GET <sitemap index>          → child sitemaps
GET <child sitemaps>         → job URLs + <lastmod>
  filter: lastmod > last_run  ← free incremental crawling
GET <job URL>                → parse <script type="application/ld+json">
                               → JobPosting object, done
```

This is one plain HTTP request per posting. No browser, no JS execution, no rendering. It is roughly two orders of magnitude cheaper than Playwright and it is what the site is built to serve.

`<lastmod>` gives you change detection for free: you re-fetch only what moved. Combined with `If-Modified-Since` / `ETag` conditional requests, steady-state cost collapses to near zero after the initial backfill.

**Reliability note:** <cite index="105-1">Web Data Commons found that title and datePosted appear in 99% of JSON-LD job postings, while baseSalary and employmentType appear in roughly 80%.</cite> So core fields are dependable; treat the optional ones as missing-at-random and report their coverage rate per source.

**Add `extend/schema.org` field capture.** The vocabulary includes `skills`, `qualifications`, `experienceRequirements` and `educationRequirements`. Where a board populates these, you get a structured skills signal without any NLP at all — a second ground truth alongside hh.ru's `key_skills` and the Swedish enrichment.

### B.2 The backing JSON API

Most job boards built in the last several years are single-page apps whose public frontend calls an internal REST or GraphQL endpoint. That endpoint serves the same unauthenticated public data, already as JSON.

Open the network tab on a search results page, find the XHR, and use it directly. This is not circumvention — it is the same public data by a cleaner path, and it removes your dependency on brittle DOM selectors.

**Highly likely to apply:** NoFluffJobs, justjoin.it, Welcome to the Jungle, Wellfound, Cutshort, Instahyre, OfferZen, Findy, LAPRAS.

Two rules: never send an `Authorization` header you obtained by creating an account, and honour whatever rate limit the endpoint signals.

### B.3 RSS and legacy feeds

Older boards frequently still expose `/rss`, `/feed`, or `?format=rss` per search query. Low volume, but a free change-notification channel that keeps your incremental crawl honest. Worth a five-minute check per board. Likely on APEC, Careers24, Job Mail, doda.

---

## Route C — Managed vendors

Use where A and B fail. The point is not just capability — it is that the vendor **assumes the collection liability**, which is what makes a hostile source tractable for an academic project that cannot itself run an adversarial crawling operation.

| Vendor | Best for | Note |
|---|---|---|
| **Bright Data** | Naukri, Indeed, LinkedIn, PNet | Datasets + Web Scraper API. Prefer their pre-collected datasets over driving proxies yourself — see the ethics point in the earlier discussion |
| **Apify** | Long tail of specific boards | Store has per-board actors; check coverage board by board. Per-result pricing suits a bounded research pull |
| **TheirStack** | ATS-sourced, deduplicated | Cross-source dedup is its differentiator, and dedup is load-bearing for your percentages |
| **Coresignal** | LinkedIn-adjacent at volume | Deduplicated records |
| **Oxylabs** | Alternative to Bright Data | Comparable products, worth a quote |

**Procurement tip:** buy a **field subset**, not full records. Cheaper, and excluding recruiter-contact fields at purchase time discharges your GDPR minimisation obligation at the boundary rather than after ingestion.

---

## Route D — Official request

Genuinely under-used, and it costs one email. Boards routinely grant academic access and almost nobody asks.

**Ask these, in this order of likely success:**

- **OfferZen** — already publishes a methodologically documented developer survey. They are small, research-friendly, and their commercial interest aligns with being cited in a study of developer skill demand. High probability of a yes.
- **Habr Career**, **Findy**, **Forkwell**, **paiza** — all already publish their own skills/salary analyses. They have the data assembled and a reason to want academic validation.
- **NoFluffJobs / justjoin.it** — their structured must-have/nice-to-have data is their product differentiator; framed as a citation opportunity this is a reasonable ask.
- **JCSE (Wits)** — an academic peer; ask for the ICT Skills Survey microdata, not just the report.

Send from an institutional address, state the research question, offer co-authorship or acknowledgement, and commit to publishing aggregates only. A single yes here beats months of scraping engineering.

---

